{"parentUuid":"165cc942-3b73-4783-8057-f80016b99d38","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01F8pbzxPcUynoBQXCxGB14P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, fix it. The reason is exactly what you're pointing at — \"new terminal\" being the difference is a *symptom*, not an explanation, and a feature whose correctness depends on which terminal you launched it from is broken even when it appears to work.\n\nWhat \"new terminal\" actually rules out\n\nYour old shell had something in its environment that the new one didn't. Two candidates account for almost all \"works in fresh shell\" cases:\n\n1. **Stale `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` from a prior aborted run** — if you ever exported these manually for debugging, or a previous `entire review` left them set in your shell (it shouldn't, but a crashed parent could), `os.Environ()` in the new run picks them up. `AppendReviewEnv` (env.go:97-101) tries to defend against this by stripping pre-existing `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` keys before re-adding them, so this path *should* be safe — but only for those specific keys.\n\n2. **Ambient state from a parent agent process** — your old terminal was inside or downstream of an active claude-code session (likely `6a33efd5`, which the logs show was alive all day). When that session shells out, it injects its own `CLAUDE_*` markers and may filter or replace env. If anything in that chain dropped or rewrote a var the spawn path needed (PATH, HOME, GIT_*), `paths.WorktreeRoot(ctx)` or `git rev-parse HEAD` inside `entire review` would resolve differently than they do in your new terminal — and `cfg.StartingSHA` would end up empty or wrong, blowing the SHA check at `lifecycle.go:1052` silently.\n\nYou can identify which one by running this in your *old* terminal (don't open a new one first):\n\n```fish\nenv | grep -E '^(ENTIRE_|CLAUDE_|GIT_|PATH=|HOME=|PWD=)' | sort\n```\n\nCompare to the same command in a fresh terminal. The diff is the proximate cause.\n\nWhy this needs fixing regardless\n\nThe bug isn't \"stale env in shell\" — that's a user-state issue. The bug is that **the failure mode is invisible**. Three small fixes would make this self-diagnosing instead of \"open an issue and pray someone reproduces it\":\n\n1. **lifecycle.go:1042** — replace the silent return with a debug log: `logging.Debug(ctx, \"review env adoption skipped: ENTIRE_REVIEW_SESSION not set\", ...)`. Five lines. Today's whole hour-long debugging session collapses to one grep.\n\n2. **cmd.go:653** — when `manifest.Sources == 0`, print a *user-visible* warning to stderr (not just debug log): `\"warning: review completed but session was not tagged as a review; --findings will not see this run. Run with ENTIRE_LOG_LEVEL=debug to diagnose.\"` That single line means the next user hits the same bug and can self-help instead of opening \"feature doesn't work\" with no logs.\n\n3. **`AppendReviewEnv` is too narrow** — it strips stale `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` but doesn't probe whether the env actually arrives at the hook. A simple defense: have `entire review` write a sentinel file at `<git-common-dir>/entire-review/.handshake-<run-id>` containing `{run_id, starting_sha, agent}`, set `ENTIRE_REVIEW_RUN=<run-id>` on the spawned agent, and at hook time look up the sentinel by run-id env var. If the env var arrived → use the env path. If not → fall back to \"look for any sentinel younger than this hook's SessionStart that names this agent.\" Belt-and-suspenders, costs ~30 lines, and the marker file is debuggable when env propagation fails (you can `cat` it and see exactly what the parent set).\n\nThe CLAUDE.md anti-features list explicitly bans the `PendingReviewMarker` for launchable agents because the env-var design \"makes it unnecessary.\" That was true on a clean process tree. It's not true under nested agent invocations. The marker isn't a regression — it's a fallback for when the elegant primary path can't deliver.\n\nIf you want to file these, fix #1 alone is high-leverage and tiny. #2 prevents the silent-failure UX you just hit. #3 is bigger and worth a brainstorm on whether the marker fallback for non-launchable agents could be reused as a defense-in-depth for launchable ones too.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- \"Works in a fresh terminal\" is a load-bearing diagnostic clue for env-propagation bugs, but it's also a tell that the feature has an undeclared precondition: *the ambient environment must be clean*. Features should declare their preconditions in code (validate, fail loudly) or eliminate the dependency on them. Silently degrading when a precondition isn't met is the worst outcome — the feature appears to run, costs full agent tokens, and produces nothing.\n- The fact that #1145's tests all pass but the feature breaks under realistic invocation is a useful generalization: when a feature's contract spans process boundaries, *unit tests on either side of the boundary are insufficient by construction* — only end-to-end tests that actually fork the boundary can verify it. A 30-line addition to the Vogon canary that runs `entire review --agent vogon` and asserts a manifest appears would have caught this on the merging PR. Cheap insurance.\n- The \"elegant primary, ugly fallback\" pattern (env var primary, marker file fallback) is uncomfortable but correct for cross-process contracts. It mirrors how TCP works (clean connection state primary, RST/timeout fallback) — you can't ship a real-world feature that only works when the network is perfect, and you can't ship a real-world feature that only works when env propagation is perfect. The CLAUDE.md anti-features section is sound design advice for the happy path; it's wrong about the unhappy path.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1356,"cache_read_input_tokens":160015,"output_tokens":408