{"parentUuid":"84f31ebb-4fb4-4063-a3ae-40f4be0868f4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Wi5cRnYmS5Z7ceVDvjiwcY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Correct. On a working review, the warning never fires — output is identical to today. The user sees the same agent narratives, the same synthesis verdict, the same \"Review complete. To apply all review findings: ...\" footer. No new lines, no new prompts, no behavioral changes.\n\nThe warning is strictly a failure-path feature. It fires only when one of three things goes wrong inside `writePostReviewManifest`:\n\n1. Loading session state from disk fails (system error)\n2. Sessions exist but none are tagged as reviews (today's bug — handshake didn't take)\n3. Writing the manifest file fails (disk full, permissions, etc.)\n\nAll three are silent today. All three become visible after this fix. None of them happen on a normal working run.\n\nSo the change is asymmetric on purpose: zero noise added when things work, useful guidance added when they don't. That's the entire UX delta.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The good shape of this fix is what makes it mergeable on its own merits. Reviewers can convince themselves quickly that it can't regress the happy path — every new line of output is gated behind an existing failure check that today already returns silently. There's no scenario where this change makes a working review noisier or behave differently. That property (zero risk to the success path) is what lets observability fixes ship fast, ahead of the riskier root-cause work.\n- The asymmetry — silence on success, signal on failure — is also what distinguishes a *good* warning from a *spammy* one. Many codebases accumulate warnings that print on every invocation \"just in case,\" and users learn to tune them out. A warning that only fires when the stated invariant has actually been violated retains its signal value. Users who ne