zero regression on the path that actually matters to users.\n\n**The failure-path smoke test is not worth running.** It would require forcing the env handshake to break (running from the contaminated terminal context, which we can't reliably reproduce, or patching the spawn code to deliberately drop env vars). The wrapper test already proves the warning fires from production code; eyeballing it in a deliberately broken run adds no information.\n\nIf the smoke run shows the happy path intact, ship it. If something looks wrong (warning prints when it shouldn't, footer missing when it should be there), we have a small surface to dig into before merging.\n\nOptional but worth a half-second's thought: read the smoke test's terminal output and gut-check the warning text. The wording I chose (\"env-var handshake did not reach the hook\") is precise but jargony. If it reads weirdly to you in context, easy fix — change the string in `cmd.go:warnManifestNotWritten` and re-run the unit test, takes 30 seconds. That kind of copy iteration is the one thing this PR can't do without your eyes on it.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The pattern here — automated tests prove correctness, manual smoke tests prove ergonomics — is worth naming. Unit/integration tests are excellent at catching \"does this code do what I claim,\" they're terrible at catching \"does this code feel right to a user.\" A 30-second eyeball of real output catches things tests will never catch (visual layout, copy clarity, ordering surprises) and is the appropriate complement to automated coverage rather than a replacement for it.\n- The choice to *not* smoke-test the failure path is a real one worth defending in PR review. The temptation is \"we should test every path before shipping.\" The reality is: the wrapper test already exercises production code through the failure branch, and forcing a real failure requires either flaky reproduction (the original 12:36 issue we couldn't reliably re